steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Space, Time, and Energy}

      SW developers conventionally characterize their pro&shy;grams in terms of _time_ versus _space_ &ndash; a classic trade-off.

      But when targeting HW with limited power sources, we must also measure our programs&rsquo; _energy_ consumption.

      Consider `SleepyRunnerP`&thinsp;{[tr,102/07/03]} as a case in point:

      {[DC,1]} **EM&bull;Builder** itself reports code&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;data size [bytes]<br>
      {[DC,2]} **Logic 2** measures execution time [&mu;s] {[tr,102/08/02]}<br>
      {[DC,3]} **Joulescope Viewer** shows power over time [&mu;J]

      Note how {[dc,2]} and {[dc,3]} reveal different perspectives on the _same_ burst of active work &ndash; a single **CoreMark** iteration.

      <div class="em-info">

      The next tour will formally introduce [Joulescope](https://www.joulescope.com/).

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,splitscreen]&thinsp;Side-by-Side Comparison}

      We can execute `SleepyRunnerP` on any target HW whose EMS distro package supports sleep&thinsp;+&thinsp;wakeup.&thinsp; {[tr,103/07/01]}

      Using the **Joulescope Viewer**, we've zoomed in on a single algorithmic burst running under three different distros.

      Compare the width and height of each active burst:

      burst {[dc,2]} &mdash; returns to sleep earliest with lowest `avg mW` <br>
      burst {[dc,1]} &mdash; takes longer to sleep with highest `avg mW`<br>
      burst {[dc,3]} &mdash; returns to sleep last but with good `avg mW`<br>

      Clearly, `nordic.nrf54` consumes the least amount of energy over this 20&thinsp;ms window of time.

      <div class="em-info">

      But what about `nordic.nrf52` versus `ti.cc23xx`&thinsp;??

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp;See the Pattern}

      Earlier tours used `DbgB` logic traces to distinquish active execution periods from lite or deep sleep.&thinsp; {[tr,102/06/04]}

      With **Joulescope Viewer** we can eyeball the height of the power trace to distinquish periods of work from sleep:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&thinsp; WORK &mdash; the MCU draws milliwatts of power<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&thinsp; SLEEP &mdash; the MCU draws microwatts of power<br>

      As you'll appreciate, the WORK&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;SLEEP power averages have significant impact on total energy consumption.

      <div class="em-info">

      But what if the MCU only wakes from sleep (say) every _minute_ &ndash; can we project the energy efficiency gain&thinsp;??

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,measuring_tape]&thinsp;What Does One Event Cost?}

      Let&rsquo;s zoom in on a single burst of activity from an earlier capture &ndash; quantifying the energy cost of this event.

      For the purpose of comparison, we've conservatively cho&shy;sen a 20&thinsp;ms window &ndash; ample time to complete WORK.

      The extra SLEEP margin does lower avg mW {[dc,1]} &ndash; but adds little to the event’s _cost_ of 91.87 µJ {[dc,2]}.

      💡&thinsp;HW draws power &ndash; but SW shapes energy over time.

      Once we return to SLEEP (with SW _inactive_), `avg mW` {[dc, 3]} settles &ndash; allowing use to project energy consumption.

      <div class="em-info">

      But how much does SLEEP impact energy efficiency over a one-second event cycle&thinsp;?? &nbsp; Or a one-minute cycle&thinsp;??

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,cycle]&thinsp;The Whole Cycle Counts}

